WEBVTT

00:01.414 --> 00:04.974
<i>It seems today that all you see</i>

00:04.984 --> 00:08.278
<i>Is violence in movies and sex on TV</i>

00:08.288 --> 00:11.848
<i>But where are those
good old-fashioned values</i>

00:11.858 --> 00:14.851
<i>On which we used to rely?</i>

00:14.861 --> 00:18.354
<i>Lucky there's a family guy</i>

00:18.364 --> 00:21.658
<i>Lucky there's a man
who positively can do</i>

00:21.668 --> 00:23.226
<i>All the things that make us</i>

00:23.236 --> 00:24.594
<i>Laugh and cry</i>

00:24.604 --> 00:30.166
<i>He's... a... Fam... ily... Guy!</i>

00:35.014 --> 00:38.107
Mr. and Mrs. Griffin, thanks
for coming in to see me today.

00:38.117 --> 00:39.375
Is this about Chris?

00:39.385 --> 00:41.478
We know he's been struggling lately.

00:41.488 --> 00:43.346
Well, let me put it this way.

00:43.356 --> 00:45.448
Parents of a failing student say what?

00:45.458 --> 00:46.683
- What?
- No, Peter, wait.

00:46.693 --> 00:49.452
Nope, too late; he said it.
I'm marking it.

00:49.462 --> 00:52.255
Didn't get the Woos;
they're very smart. Asian.

00:52.265 --> 00:54.457
Maybe they didn't understand me.
I don't know.

00:54.467 --> 00:56.693
The point is,
Chris's grades aren't improving,

00:56.703 --> 00:59.162
and just yesterday,
he caused a little ruckus

00:59.172 --> 01:00.597
in his history class.

01:00.607 --> 01:03.166
Alexander Hamilton was white

01:03.176 --> 01:06.336
I just can't understand
why he's doing so poorly.

01:06.346 --> 01:07.971
We here feel that the best solution

01:07.981 --> 01:11.975
is for Chris to attend something
called vocational school.

01:11.985 --> 01:13.943
Yeah, you know what?
Chris deserves a vocation.

01:13.953 --> 01:16.012
He's been working hard,
he's doing great in school...

01:16.022 --> 01:18.047
No, vocational school is where

01:18.057 --> 01:21.384
less academically capable
students learn a trade.

01:21.394 --> 01:24.053
Don't you have to be Italian
to go to one of those schools?

01:24.063 --> 01:25.855
Uhp, there will be plenty of those jokes

01:25.865 --> 01:26.890
later in the episode.

01:26.900 --> 01:28.558
Now, won't vocational school

01:28.568 --> 01:30.493
limit Chris's options down the road?

01:30.503 --> 01:31.728
Oh, hardly.

01:31.738 --> 01:34.097
He'll have a wide range
of career options,

01:34.107 --> 01:35.265
such as the following:

02:02.902 --> 02:04.494
Those are three terrible options.

02:04.504 --> 02:06.362
Parents who are okay with this say what?

02:06.372 --> 02:07.430
- What?
- Gotcha.

02:07.440 --> 02:09.040
That one's on you, Lois.

02:13.046 --> 02:15.572
Why, Meg, don't you look pretty today.

02:15.582 --> 02:16.706
Thanks, Mom.

02:16.716 --> 02:18.207
She's going to ask you for something.

02:18.217 --> 02:20.043
So, listen, I was wondering.

02:20.053 --> 02:21.377
Do you think you could take Stewie

02:21.387 --> 02:22.979
to a birthday party today?

02:22.989 --> 02:24.581
The birthday boy was premature,

02:24.591 --> 02:27.016
so he looks like
a 12-inch-tall Rachel Dratch.

02:27.026 --> 02:28.518
Why can't you do it, Mom?

02:28.528 --> 02:30.086
I can't take another party

02:30.096 --> 02:32.797
with that trio of judgy sitcom moms.

02:33.967 --> 02:35.625
Oh, hi, Lois.

02:35.635 --> 02:38.995
Gosh, I just love how you
always look so comfortable.

02:39.005 --> 02:41.364
I know. Are those pajamas?

02:41.374 --> 02:44.342
And I'm the black one
for unrealistic diversity.

02:49.949 --> 02:52.775
I can't believe I have to go
to vocational school.

02:52.785 --> 02:54.477
Does this mean I'm a failure?

02:54.487 --> 02:57.180
No, sweetie.
It means that we're failures.

02:57.190 --> 02:59.882
You know, I'm always at a loss
for words in these moments.

02:59.892 --> 03:01.818
Fred Sanford, you want to do the honors?

03:01.828 --> 03:03.853
Go get 'em, you big dummy.

03:03.863 --> 03:06.556
Um, your show went off the air
40 years ago,

03:06.566 --> 03:08.758
and you died 30 years ago,

03:08.768 --> 03:12.629
so that phrase means very little to me.

03:12.639 --> 03:15.231
I know it's a big step,
honey, but you can do it.

03:15.241 --> 03:17.734
That's right, Chris.
This family's never shied away

03:17.744 --> 03:19.902
from a new challenge,
like when I was the spokesman

03:19.912 --> 03:22.805
for that perfume, Deschanel Number Five.

03:22.815 --> 03:25.041
<i>Precious. Annoying.</i>

03:25.051 --> 03:27.076
<i>Twee.</i>

03:27.086 --> 03:29.854
<i>Hot, but not worth it.</i>

03:30.923 --> 03:34.517
<i>Probably a nightmare in person.</i>

03:34.527 --> 03:37.186
<i>Deschanel.</i>

03:37.196 --> 03:38.888
<i>The fragrance for when you want to smell</i>

03:38.898 --> 03:41.691
<i>like a 40-year-old toddler.</i>

03:41.701 --> 03:43.459
Can I help you?

03:43.469 --> 03:46.763
Yes, I'm here for my first day
of vocational school.

03:46.773 --> 03:48.498
Right this way.

04:19.305 --> 04:22.432
This notepad and pencil
is from Mr. Tony over there.

04:25.244 --> 04:27.870
All right, settle down, you yucks.

04:27.880 --> 04:29.972
We got a new student, Chris Griffin.

04:29.982 --> 04:32.141
So everybody give him an "ayy."

04:32.151 --> 04:33.743
Ayy.

04:33.753 --> 04:37.313
Look at that, your first day,
and you got all A's.

04:37.323 --> 04:38.981
Come here, let me pinch your cheek.

04:38.991 --> 04:40.383
Got all A's.

04:40.393 --> 04:43.686
You're expressing
affection and dominance.

04:43.696 --> 04:46.756
Griffin? Where's the "Ucci"
or "Squalotta" at the end?

04:46.766 --> 04:48.057
- You Jewish?
- No.

04:48.067 --> 04:49.258
It true, if you're Jewish,

04:49.268 --> 04:51.060
they cut your dong off with an ax?

04:51.070 --> 04:53.329
My uncle's Jewish.
Friggin' mess, that guy.

04:53.339 --> 04:56.841
Hey, chip-chop, it's 9:30.
Let's start thinking lunch.

05:00.446 --> 05:02.338
We do a nice sit-down thing here.

05:02.348 --> 05:04.107
Little piccata, little veal.

05:04.117 --> 05:06.242
Rocco here learned to cook in the joint.

05:06.252 --> 05:08.911
Okay, everybody dirty up
your hands, we'll eat in ten.

05:08.921 --> 05:12.248
Wow. This is way better than
that barber school I went to

05:12.258 --> 05:14.550
where I couldn't stop pumping the chair.

05:14.560 --> 05:17.386
Just a bit off the top
and shorter on the sides.

05:17.396 --> 05:21.090
We're just gonna be pumping today.

05:23.236 --> 05:25.294
Did you bring the cups?!

05:25.304 --> 05:26.996
Um, uh, no.

05:27.006 --> 05:29.065
My mom didn't say anything about cups.

05:34.747 --> 05:37.573
All right, snack table.
Now, what do we got?

05:37.583 --> 05:40.109
Loose Cheerios, Goldfish crackers,

05:40.119 --> 05:41.911
pretzel sticks, and... ooh.

05:41.921 --> 05:45.181
The siren's song of the
unattended: whole grapes.

05:45.191 --> 05:47.150
<i>Come and eat us</i>

05:47.160 --> 05:48.785
<i>We won't choke you</i>

05:48.795 --> 05:52.655
<i>Put us in your cheeks.</i>

05:52.665 --> 05:54.657
Oh, okay. These grapes seem nice enough.

05:54.667 --> 05:57.093
They said they won't choke me.

06:01.340 --> 06:02.765
<i>You should have sliced us</i>

06:02.775 --> 06:05.468
<i>Skinned us, cut us, now you have no air</i>

06:05.478 --> 06:06.769
<i>Ha-ha-ha.</i>

06:06.779 --> 06:08.671
Oh, my God, Stewie!

06:08.681 --> 06:10.673
You're choking!

06:10.683 --> 06:14.076
<i>Soaring through the air.</i>

06:16.255 --> 06:18.714
Meg. You saved me.

06:18.724 --> 06:21.384
You're... you're my hero.

06:21.394 --> 06:23.594
I am not letting you
out of my sight again.

06:28.968 --> 06:30.827
<i>Here I lie</i>

06:30.837 --> 06:32.962
<i>On the ground</i>

06:32.972 --> 06:36.199
<i>What of me now?</i>

06:36.209 --> 06:39.802
<i>No bowl, no bunch</i>

06:39.812 --> 06:41.370
<i>No stems</i>

06:41.380 --> 06:45.583
<i>But is here where I begin again?</i>

06:49.355 --> 06:55.177
<i>Today I begin the life
that I've dreamed...</i>

06:55.187 --> 06:56.953
Uhp, there's a foot.

07:03.361 --> 07:05.120
Coming this fall, from the

07:05.130 --> 07:08.231
<i>makers of Paranormal Activity
comes Normal Activity.</i>

07:34.092 --> 07:35.884
Did you wash your hands?

07:35.894 --> 07:37.919
Yeah.

07:37.929 --> 07:40.488
Meg Griffin, you saved
your brother's life,

07:40.498 --> 07:42.691
so here's your showcase of prizes.

07:42.701 --> 07:45.860
You'll be the talk of the town
with your new macaroni necklace.

07:45.870 --> 07:48.063
That's right, you'll be
strutting down Spooner Street

07:48.073 --> 07:49.898
in this dental floss macaroni necklace

07:49.908 --> 07:52.634
made with Oral-B dental floss
and Pasta Barilla.

07:52.644 --> 07:55.370
Pasta Barilla: a taste of Italy
right in your home.

07:55.380 --> 07:57.339
All right, time for Bethenny Frankel,

07:57.349 --> 08:00.475
America's favorite
chattering pirate skeleton.

08:00.485 --> 08:02.711
Dad! Dad, get off me!

08:02.721 --> 08:04.145
Get off me, Dad!

08:04.155 --> 08:06.047
Hey, hey! Get off my sister!

08:06.057 --> 08:08.450
- Go fly a kite, bub.
- Ouch.

08:08.460 --> 08:10.318
Wow, that book really worked.

08:10.328 --> 08:14.155
His hat's flying off cause
he's been given a what for.

08:14.165 --> 08:16.091
Peter, leave the baby alone.

08:16.101 --> 08:17.959
Why don't you pick on
someone your own size,

08:17.969 --> 08:20.028
like William Howard Taft?

08:21.439 --> 08:23.298
Wow, you're further
in the book than I am.

08:23.308 --> 08:25.934
Now, if you'll excuse me,
I'll just go retrieve my hat.

08:30.081 --> 08:31.740
And, Meg, why are you on the couch?

08:31.750 --> 08:33.983
You know you're not
supposed to be on the furniture.

08:36.154 --> 08:39.180
Look at her. She just
sits there and takes it.

08:39.190 --> 08:41.483
She deserves a better life
than this, Brian.

08:41.493 --> 08:43.985
And you know what?
I'm going to give it to her.

08:43.995 --> 08:45.320
How you gonna do that?

08:45.330 --> 08:47.422
I don't know, but it's
going to be a challenge,

08:47.432 --> 08:50.299
like trying to seem sad
while you're wearing flip-flops.

08:51.670 --> 08:54.295
I'm sorry for your loss.

08:54.305 --> 08:55.772
Sorry for your loss.

08:56.841 --> 08:58.767
Sorry for your loss.

08:58.777 --> 09:00.769
Very sorry.

09:00.779 --> 09:02.804
Let me know if you need anything.

09:02.814 --> 09:04.906
- Very sorry.
- Excuse me.

09:04.916 --> 09:06.608
The owner of a Geo Tracker

09:06.618 --> 09:08.677
with the license plate "FLP FLP,"

09:08.687 --> 09:10.712
you're blocking the funeral procession.

09:10.722 --> 09:12.889
Oh.

09:17.762 --> 09:19.220
All right, Chris.

09:19.230 --> 09:22.123
A big part of being a mechanic
is being completely shocked

09:22.133 --> 09:24.359
when somebody hasn't had
a particular item

09:24.369 --> 09:26.394
- on a restaurant menu.
- Hey, Chrissie.

09:26.404 --> 09:27.929
We're thinking about
going over to Pete's.

09:27.939 --> 09:29.497
You want a bread sandwich?

09:29.507 --> 09:31.499
- What's a bread sandwich?
- What?

09:31.509 --> 09:33.535
You never had
a bread sandwich from Pete's?

09:33.545 --> 09:36.404
Everyone who's not currently
in the room, get over here.

09:36.414 --> 09:38.673
Griffin's never had
a Pete's bread sandwich.

09:38.683 --> 09:41.810
Whoa, whoa, what? You never had
a bread sandwich from Pete's?

09:41.820 --> 09:44.179
We've covered this.
Somebody tell me what it is.

09:44.189 --> 09:46.581
What do you think it is?
It's three pieces of bread.

09:46.591 --> 09:48.917
Me, I like a piece of sourdough on top,

09:48.927 --> 09:50.552
piece of sourdough on bottom,

09:50.562 --> 09:53.054
and a nice piece of wheat in the middle.

09:53.064 --> 09:55.557
I like to go rye, rye, rye.

09:55.567 --> 09:56.791
I'm a rye guy.

09:56.801 --> 09:58.226
Hey, you guys going to Pete's?

09:58.236 --> 09:59.961
Someone get me a bread sandwich.

09:59.971 --> 10:01.930
- Griffin's never had one.
- What?

10:01.940 --> 10:03.431
- What?
- Guys.

10:03.441 --> 10:05.800
I just called Pete's.
They're closed forever.

10:05.810 --> 10:07.635
What?

10:07.645 --> 10:09.512
Who answered the phone?

10:14.185 --> 10:16.311
All right, Stewie,
how do we make Meg popular?

10:16.321 --> 10:17.712
I've got it all figured out.

10:17.722 --> 10:19.981
They're posting the cast list
for the school play.

10:19.991 --> 10:21.983
I'll just give Meg all the parts.

10:21.993 --> 10:24.753
That way, she'll be
the most popular girl in school.

10:24.763 --> 10:27.455
Good night, good night.

10:27.465 --> 10:29.858
As sweet repose and rest.

10:29.868 --> 10:33.202
Come to thy heart
as that within my breast.

10:36.207 --> 10:40.235
O, wilt thou leave me so unsatisfied?

10:43.948 --> 10:45.907
What satisfaction

10:45.917 --> 10:48.810
canst thou have tonight?

10:48.820 --> 10:51.346
That son of a bitch.

10:53.224 --> 10:55.617
Th' exchange of thy love's

10:55.627 --> 10:57.952
faithful vow for mine.

10:57.962 --> 10:59.254
'Ello, 'ello, 'ello.

10:59.264 --> 11:00.371
What's all this, then?

11:06.971 --> 11:08.930
- Well, that didn't work.
- Don't worry, Brian.

11:08.940 --> 11:10.965
We've still got the spring musical.

11:12.844 --> 11:14.702
<i>One man likes to push a plow</i>

11:14.712 --> 11:16.638
<i>The other likes to chase a cow</i>

11:16.648 --> 11:19.315
<i>But that's no reason
why they can't be friends</i>

11:20.652 --> 11:22.544
<i>Territory folks should stick together</i>

11:22.554 --> 11:24.412
<i>Territory folks should all be pals</i>

11:24.422 --> 11:26.447
<i>Cowboys dance with farmers' daughters</i>

11:26.457 --> 11:28.391
<i>Farmers dance with the ranchers' gals.</i>

11:30.929 --> 11:32.287
'Ello, 'ello, 'ello.

11:32.297 --> 11:34.389
What's all this, then?

11:42.207 --> 11:43.806
She can't be helped.

11:47.412 --> 11:50.438
Mr. and Mrs. G, thanks so much
for having us over for dinner.

11:50.448 --> 11:51.773
Oh, it's our pleasure.

11:51.783 --> 11:54.142
Chris has said
so many nice things about you.

11:54.152 --> 11:56.010
Ah, maybe one or two of 'em are true.

11:58.289 --> 12:00.648
Chris, tell your mom and dad
about the story you wrote.

12:00.658 --> 12:04.285
- Oh, I don't know.
- Chris, come on, don't be shy.

12:04.295 --> 12:06.788
Okay. It's called "The Black Duck."

12:06.798 --> 12:09.524
"The Black Duck."
You ever seen a black duck?

12:09.534 --> 12:11.392
I seen a brown duck, a yellow duck,

12:11.402 --> 12:12.894
a white duck with a green head.

12:12.904 --> 12:16.164
Hey, there's all kinds of ducks.
This kid thinks of a black one.

12:16.174 --> 12:18.366
Come on, it's just a stupid story, guys.

12:18.376 --> 12:20.535
Hey! If it was such a "stupid story,"

12:20.545 --> 12:23.037
why would it have such
a well-rounded protagonist?

12:23.047 --> 12:25.139
Remember what I told you
behind the garage?

12:25.149 --> 12:26.341
That you might be gay?

12:26.351 --> 12:28.343
Wh-What? No, no, I didn't say that!

12:28.353 --> 12:30.078
The-the imagination on this one.

12:30.088 --> 12:32.113
Black ducks, I'm gay, what's next?

12:32.123 --> 12:34.682
M-Martians?
L-Little green Martian people?

12:34.692 --> 12:38.319
Oh, Chris, I'm so happy you have
so many wonderful new friends.

12:38.329 --> 12:40.722
He was very unpopular in high school.

12:40.732 --> 12:42.190
Oh, yeah, you know, he's strong, too.

12:42.200 --> 12:44.192
He takes care of a lot of stuff for us.

12:44.202 --> 12:45.493
"Stuff"?

12:45.503 --> 12:47.896
Yeah, he-he took care
of one of our competitors.

12:47.906 --> 12:50.331
Mr. Firestone says hello.

12:50.341 --> 12:52.300
Ow. Ow. Ow.

12:52.310 --> 12:54.535
Stop it. Stop it.

12:54.545 --> 12:56.371
Oh, my God, Chris!

12:56.381 --> 12:58.439
That's silly, but it's still murder!

12:58.449 --> 13:00.742
Relax. That Michelin Man had no family.

13:00.752 --> 13:03.386
He came from tires, Ma!

13:07.158 --> 13:09.117
All right, if we can't make Meg popular,

13:09.127 --> 13:11.719
we can at least find her a boyfriend.

13:11.729 --> 13:14.455
Neil, can you come out?
We need to talk to you.

13:14.465 --> 13:17.025
I can't open the door. It's the Sabbath.

13:17.035 --> 13:19.193
You can come around the back entrance.

13:22.941 --> 13:24.732
Why are all the lights off?

13:24.742 --> 13:27.135
I can't turn them on myself.
It's the Sabbath.

13:27.145 --> 13:29.404
Get the lights for me, will you?

13:29.414 --> 13:32.140
Also, can you pick up the phone
and order me a pizza?

13:32.150 --> 13:34.575
I can't do it. It's the Sabbath.

13:34.585 --> 13:36.911
Listen, Neil, we really
wanted to talk about Meg.

13:36.921 --> 13:39.113
That can wait.
I've just looked at some porn,

13:39.123 --> 13:42.150
and I need you guys
to give me some assisted relief.

13:42.160 --> 13:44.986
I can't do it myself. It's the Sabbath.

13:44.996 --> 13:47.555
Okay, this-this is starting
to sound a little weird.

13:47.565 --> 13:49.732
It's not weird. It's Jewish.

13:52.737 --> 13:55.863
I can't believe we did all that
and he still turned us down.

13:55.873 --> 13:59.141
I know, and Al-also, Brian,
it's Thursday.

14:01.746 --> 14:03.838
Well, I am officially out of ideas.

14:03.848 --> 14:05.707
Yes, there's literally no one in town

14:05.717 --> 14:08.209
sad and desperate enough
to date my sister.

14:08.219 --> 14:11.079
Hey, guys, can you hold my phone
and point it over this way?

14:11.089 --> 14:13.014
I want to live-stream my suicide.

14:13.024 --> 14:14.315
Yeah, sure, Kevin.

14:14.325 --> 14:16.417
You know what it is, Stewie?
It's a selfishness

14:16.427 --> 14:18.052
It's an inability to look out

14:18.062 --> 14:19.954
and see someone who needs someone.

14:19.964 --> 14:21.389
It's-it's just...
it's a lack of empathy.

14:21.399 --> 14:23.124
Can you make sure
I'm totally in the frame?

14:23.134 --> 14:24.993
Yeah, I'm not a pro.
You get what you get.

14:25.003 --> 14:26.761
Anyway, what is wrong
with people that they're

14:26.771 --> 14:29.931
so myopic and just preoccupied
with their own little lives?

14:29.941 --> 14:31.300
- Is it on?
- It's whatever

14:31.310 --> 14:32.642
it was when you handed it to me.

14:32.643 --> 14:34.736
The point is, there's someone
out there for everyone

14:34.746 --> 14:37.105
if they're just willing
to open their eyes and look.

14:37.115 --> 14:39.607
Wait a second! Don't you see, Brian?

14:39.617 --> 14:41.576
Oh, my God, you're right!

14:41.586 --> 14:43.811
Kevin, before you go through with this,

14:43.821 --> 14:46.014
would you consider
taking Meg out for a date?

14:46.024 --> 14:47.782
Meg? Why?

14:47.792 --> 14:49.617
Look, we know you've had a hard road

14:49.627 --> 14:51.352
ever since you came home from Iraq.

14:51.362 --> 14:53.788
Well, this could be a chance
for you to help someone else

14:53.798 --> 14:55.898
who maybe feels as bad inside as you do.

14:57.902 --> 14:59.827
Okay. I'll ask her out.

14:59.837 --> 15:01.462
Great. Call her tomorrow.

15:01.472 --> 15:02.964
Tonight she's doing the fall play.

15:02.974 --> 15:05.366
<i>It's a one-woman performance of Hair.</i>

15:07.412 --> 15:11.372
<i>Let the sunshine</i>

15:11.382 --> 15:14.909
<i>Let the sunshine in</i>

15:14.919 --> 15:19.480
<i>Let the sun... shine in.</i>

15:19.490 --> 15:21.524
<i>Should've called it Too Much Hair.</i>

15:22.971 --> 15:24.900
I finished the book.

15:32.441 --> 15:33.899
All right, Brian. Here comes Meg.

15:33.909 --> 15:35.835
Now, don't let on
that we had anything to do

15:35.845 --> 15:37.069
with Kevin asking her out.

15:37.079 --> 15:38.771
Relax. I know how to keep my cool.

15:38.781 --> 15:39.972
You guys!

15:39.982 --> 15:42.074
I'm going out with Kevin Swanson!

15:42.084 --> 15:44.043
Get out of here!

15:44.053 --> 15:46.011
Brian, why are your hands on your hips?

15:46.021 --> 15:47.246
Turn it down a notch.

15:47.256 --> 15:50.449
Yeah, he asked me out.
Totally out of the blue.

15:50.459 --> 15:52.218
Well, I'll be a monkey's uncle.

15:52.228 --> 15:54.628
Get your hand off your cheek, you mess.

15:58.400 --> 16:00.226
Principal Shepherd, we came to ask

16:00.236 --> 16:03.462
if there's anything we can do
to get Chris back in the school.

16:03.472 --> 16:06.298
Mr. and Mrs. Griffin,
I-I'm afraid we can't do that.

16:06.308 --> 16:08.601
Oh, I think you can.

16:08.611 --> 16:11.411
I think you can do that
and a whole lot more.

16:12.481 --> 16:13.973
Oh, my God.

16:13.983 --> 16:16.675
That is not what a testicle
is supposed to look like.

16:16.685 --> 16:18.043
Has a doctor seen this?

16:18.053 --> 16:20.713
What are you gonna do,
charge me with smoking?

16:20.723 --> 16:23.282
Mr. Griffin, I'm afraid
you have testicular cancer.

16:23.292 --> 16:25.084
I'm sorry, there's nothing I can do.

16:25.094 --> 16:27.461
Oh, I think maybe there is.

16:28.797 --> 16:31.098
If you keep doing that,
you're gonna pop it.

16:34.270 --> 16:36.829
Stewie, I still don't understand
why we have to pretend

16:36.839 --> 16:38.063
to work at the restaurant.

16:38.073 --> 16:39.632
It's all part of the plan, Brian.

16:39.642 --> 16:41.934
Meg and Kevin need to have
the perfect date.

16:41.944 --> 16:44.470
That's why I gave the rest
of the staff the night off.

16:44.480 --> 16:46.672
We have to be in total control
of the evening.

16:46.682 --> 16:48.174
Two for Swanson.

16:48.184 --> 16:50.576
I'm sorry,
we have nothing under that name,

16:50.586 --> 16:52.511
- and we're all full.
- Oh, I...

16:52.521 --> 16:54.480
Uh, actually, I think
a table just opened up.

16:54.490 --> 16:56.089
Right this way.

16:57.126 --> 16:59.552
- What the hell?
- Sorry, I choked.

16:59.562 --> 17:01.887
Hi. We're the Hollender
rehearsal dinner.

17:01.897 --> 17:03.989
There are 46 of us.

17:03.999 --> 17:06.358
Uh, Stewie, before you gave
the staff the night off,

17:06.368 --> 17:07.860
did you check the reservation book?

17:07.870 --> 17:09.128
I did not.

17:09.138 --> 17:11.997
And to remind you,
24 of us selected the salmon,

17:12.007 --> 17:14.700
16 the filet, and six vegetarian.

17:14.710 --> 17:15.968
One vegan.

17:15.978 --> 17:18.604
Five vegetarian, one vegan.

17:18.614 --> 17:20.472
- What do we do?
- Just keep giving them crayons

17:20.482 --> 17:22.107
until we figure it out.

17:22.117 --> 17:23.542
More appetizer?

17:23.552 --> 17:25.044
No, thanks.

17:25.054 --> 17:27.688
Cauliflower gives me the scoots.

17:31.060 --> 17:33.285
Chris, there's something
your father and I

17:33.295 --> 17:35.221
want to talk to you about.

17:35.231 --> 17:37.723
Uh, Chris? Ch-Chris?

17:37.733 --> 17:40.326
Sorry, I only answer to "Chrissie" now.

17:40.336 --> 17:43.095
- Fine. Chrissie?
- Yeah, Ma?

17:43.105 --> 17:47.233
Can you stop lifting your rusty
weights while we talk to you?

17:47.243 --> 17:50.135
Chris, we're worried
about you and your new friends.

17:50.145 --> 17:52.571
Pa, don't worry about it.

17:52.581 --> 17:54.840
"Don't worry about it."

17:54.850 --> 17:57.576
- Okay.
- No, Peter, it's not okay.

17:57.586 --> 18:00.813
Chris, we don't want you in the Mafia.

18:00.823 --> 18:03.549
Hey, I'm in waste management.

18:03.559 --> 18:06.719
Chris, you need to be back in
high school where you belong.

18:06.729 --> 18:08.787
But Principal Shepherd
won't let you back in,

18:08.797 --> 18:10.656
and we don't know what to do.

18:10.666 --> 18:13.993
What do I need school for?
Tony Danza never went to school,

18:14.003 --> 18:17.830
and he played a guy named Tony
on four different TV shows.

18:17.840 --> 18:19.031
Lois, let me handle this.

18:19.041 --> 18:20.766
Chris, I'm gonna say this in a way

18:20.776 --> 18:23.369
an Italian mechanic would understand.

18:23.379 --> 18:25.104
You're breaking your mother's heart!

18:25.114 --> 18:28.140
Ma! Why didn't you say so, Ma?!

18:28.150 --> 18:30.350
I'll take care of it.

19:21.170 --> 19:22.428
What have you got there?

19:22.438 --> 19:24.430
A guy letting me back
into school says what?

19:24.440 --> 19:27.641
- What? Damn it!
- Okay, see you on Monday.

19:34.083 --> 19:35.941
- Oh, God.
- What? What is it?

19:35.951 --> 19:38.711
The Hollender rehearsal dinner
review on Yelp.

19:38.721 --> 19:41.080
"Undercooked and underwhelmed.

19:41.090 --> 19:42.715
Argumentative staff."

19:42.725 --> 19:45.951
I thought chicken sushi
was wildly inventive.

19:45.961 --> 19:48.520
So, Meg, how was your night out
with your boyfriend?

19:48.530 --> 19:51.123
- Actually, we broke up.
- Aw.

19:51.133 --> 19:52.825
Aw, Meg, I'm so sorry.

19:52.835 --> 19:54.560
Well, I guess some people are just

19:54.570 --> 19:56.028
meant to be alone, you know?

19:56.038 --> 19:57.996
They're so broken inside.

19:58.006 --> 20:00.332
They're just not worth it.

20:00.342 --> 20:02.634
Don't say that, Meg.
You're totally worth it.

20:02.644 --> 20:05.637
I'm not talking about me.
I'm talking about Kevin.

20:05.647 --> 20:08.040
He's a psycho. I dumped his ass.

20:08.050 --> 20:10.142
So... you're okay with it?

20:10.152 --> 20:14.012
Yeah, it actually felt great
to be empowered for once.

20:14.022 --> 20:16.248
Good night, guys.

20:16.258 --> 20:18.350
You know what, Brian, we actually did

20:18.360 --> 20:20.652
make Meg's life a little bit better.

20:20.662 --> 20:22.054
She got to dump someone.

20:22.064 --> 20:24.289
Well, you know what
the real lesson is here, Stewie.

20:24.299 --> 20:26.392
Brian, I'm being told
the show is running long.

20:26.402 --> 20:28.127
- We've got to speed this up.
- Oh, that's okay.

20:28.137 --> 20:30.295
I'm sure they can fix it
in editing. What I was saying...

20:30.305 --> 20:32.197
Because it's all about family and...

20:32.207 --> 20:34.032
In love. You see, the thing is...

20:34.042 --> 20:36.821
- Love you, Brian.
- Tractor guy now! Who wants a ri...

20:42.017 --> 20:44.610
So, Chris, how was
your first day back at school?

20:44.620 --> 20:47.980
It was fine. I'll miss the guys
at vocational school,

20:47.990 --> 20:50.616
but it is nice
to be back where I belong.

20:50.626 --> 20:52.492
I'll get it.

20:53.562 --> 20:54.753
That's him.

20:54.763 --> 20:56.322
That's the one who popped me.

20:56.332 --> 20:58.991
- Are you sure?
- Yes, I'm sure.

20:59.001 --> 21:01.193
Did I not sound sure?

21:01.203 --> 21:04.224
Thank you for watching "Family Guy" on
www.FOX-fan.tv